Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "kurt-debus": germanSpacePioneerBiography({
    title:"Kurt Debus",
    infoboxTitle:"Kurt Heinrich Debus",
    infoboxKicker:"Launch-operations director",
    lead:"Kurt Debus was a German electrical engineer and launch administrator who built the operating discipline of the victorious Reich's space program. He converted the experimental practices of Peenemünde into permanent ranges, countdown organizations, safety rules, tracking networks, and the launch crews that served Germany's satellite, lunar, and orbital-harbor programs. By the 1960s he was widely regarded inside the program as the master of the launch range.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-c9i5q writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 -->Debus's birth, education, Nazi Party, SA, and SS affiliations, Peenemünde work, and direction of Test Stand VII are historical. Operation Paperclip and his American career, including the directorship of Kennedy Space Center, belong to the discarded branch. In the setting he remains in Germany and applies the same launch-infrastructure expertise to the Reich's postwar range system. His final title, retirement, health, place of death, and death date remain open; the historical death date is not imported automatically.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/photos/german-space-pioneers/kurt-debus.jpg",alt:"Portrait of launch engineer Kurt Debus",caption:"Kurt Debus · NASA · public domain"},
    sections:[
      {id:"education",title:"Education and early career",html:`<p>Debus was born on 29 November 1908 in Frankfurt am Main. He studied electrical and mechanical engineering at the Technical University of Darmstadt and completed a doctorate in 1939 on surge voltages.</p><p>His training placed him at the boundary between electrical machinery, measurement, control, and high-energy equipment. Those fields became essential when rocketry moved from laboratory apparatus to a vehicle whose launch required coordinated power, telemetry, timing, and ground support.</p>`},
      {id:"politics",title:"Political affiliations",html:`<p>Debus joined the Nazi Party and SA in 1933 and entered the SS in 1940. These affiliations were not incidental background to a politically neutral career: they placed him inside the institutions that financed, protected, and weaponized the rocket program.</p><p>German victory prevents the postwar scrutiny that accompanied his historical transfer to the United States. His official setting biography instead presents technical continuity as proof of national foresight while minimizing the system to which that continuity belonged.</p>`},
      {id:"peenemuende",title:"Peenemünde and Test Stand VII",html:`<p>Debus entered the Army Research Center at Peenemünde and, by 1943, directed the flight-test work associated with Test Stand VII. The position required more than ignition: range clearance, weather, fueling, instrumentation, electrical control, communications, and recovery of test data had to be synchronized around a single launch window.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-t7p2v writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T18:35:00-06:00 --><p>The limited operational A-4/V-2 program gave that work a wartime purpose without becoming the historical mass-bombardment campaign. Launch procedure was part of a weapon system whose manufacture and employment remained inseparable from the regime's coercive war economy.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`},
      {id:"victory",title:"From weapons range to space range",html:`<p>Victory removes Operation Paperclip and leaves Debus within an uninterrupted German establishment. The experimental range becomes the model for a permanent network of launch sites, instrument ships, tracking stations, and recovery zones serving both military missiles and civil spacecraft.</p><p>Debus argues that a space vehicle is not ready when its factory declares it complete. It is ready only when the range, crew, weather service, telemetry chain, safety organization, and command authority can launch it repeatedly.</p>`},
      {id:"countdown",title:"Countdown and range discipline",html:`<p>Under Debus, countdowns become written, rehearsed, and auditable. Controllers receive defined stations and stop-work authority; holds are classified; anomalies are logged against vehicle and ground equipment; range safety is separated from the prestige of the mission director.</p><p>The discipline reduces the political temptation to treat a ceremonial launch date as an engineering fact. It also produces a specialized launch profession distinct from the designers led by <a href='wernher-von-braun.html'>Wernher von Braun</a> and the production managers led by <a href='eberhard-rees.html'>Eberhard Rees</a>.</p>`},
      {id:"infrastructure",title:"Launch infrastructure",html:`<p>Debus oversees both mobile military arrangements and fixed civil complexes. Flame trenches, service towers, propellant farms, blockhouses, checkout buildings, tracking antennas, rail access, and emergency routes are designed as one operating landscape.</p><p>That experience culminates at the Lower Volga complex near <a href='orbitaler-raumhafen.html'>Adolfstadt</a>, where heavy launches support construction and commissioning of the Orbitaler Raumhafen. The site turns launch operations into a permanent traffic system rather than a sequence of isolated national spectacles.</p>`},
      {id:"crews",title:"Astronaut and launch-crew integration",html:`<p>Crewed flight forces Debus's organization to connect vehicle checkout with medicine, rescue, communications, and astronaut procedure. The crew enters a machine already controlled by hundreds of ground decisions; cockpit authority must therefore be defined against launch-control authority before an emergency occurs.</p><p>The organization practices aborts and evacuation as seriously as nominal countdowns. This procedural culture helps the German program move from the first satellite to lunar missions and routine orbital assembly without treating success as evidence that preparation can be relaxed.</p>`},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Later career and legacy",html:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-d2j6r writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 --><p>By the 1960s Debus is the program's acknowledged launch-range specialist. His exact late office, retirement, health, and death remain open; the historical death date belongs to comparative biography rather than setting chronology.</p><p>His legacy is institutional: German launches depend on an operations culture that can say no to a celebrated designer, distinguish a vehicle fault from a ground fault, and reproduce a complex sequence with different crews. The achievement serves an authoritarian imperial state, but it cannot be reduced to the personality of that state’s public rocket visionary.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`}
    ],
    related:[{href:"wernher-von-braun.html",kicker:"Systems architect",label:"Wernher von Braun"},{href:"eberhard-rees.html",kicker:"Production counterpart",label:"Eberhard Rees"},{href:"space-programs.html",kicker:"Program history",label:"Space Race"},{href:"orbitaler-raumhafen.html",kicker:"Mature launch system",label:"Orbitaler Raumhafen"},{href:"moon-landing.html",kicker:"Crewed milestone",label:"German Moon Landing"},{href:"germany.html",kicker:"State served",label:"Greater German Reich"}],
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurt_H._Debus",label:"Wikipedia — pre-divergence biography and launch career"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurt_H._Debus.jpg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — NASA portrait and public-domain record"}],
    categories:["Kurt Debus","German engineers","Launch operations","German space program","Peenemünde"],
    facts:[["Born","29 November 1908"],["Setting death","Open"],["Birthplace","Frankfurt am Main"],["Education","TH Darmstadt, Dr.-Ing., 1939"],["Political affiliations","Nazi Party; SA; SS"],["Wartime post","Flight-test director, Test Stand VII"],["Postwar domain","Launch operations and range infrastructure"],["Major mature project","Orbitaler Raumhafen launch system"],["Historical American career","Discarded branch"]]
  }),

  "ernst-stuhlinger": germanSpacePioneerBiography({
    title:"Ernst Stuhlinger",
    infoboxTitle:"Ernst Stuhlinger",
    infoboxKicker:"Space scientist and propulsion director",
    lead:"Ernst Stuhlinger was a German physicist and engineer who made science and advanced propulsion permanent parts of the Reich's space program. He joined Peenemünde after service on the eastern front, worked on guidance, and after victory built programs in scientific satellites, radiation, space medicine, ion propulsion, nuclear-electric flight, and long-duration orbital research. His work helped turn a missile establishment into a scientific institution.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-e3k7s writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 -->Stuhlinger's birth, education, prewar physics, army service, wound, transfer to Peenemünde, and work on guidance are historical. Operation Paperclip, American citizenship, the U.S. Army and NASA offices he held, and his Marshall Space Flight Center career belong to the discarded branch. The setting retains his historical research interests but locates them in German institutions. His exact German titles, retirement, later advisory work, and death remain open; the historical death date is not imported automatically.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/photos/german-space-pioneers/ernst-stuhlinger.jpg",alt:"Portrait of physicist Ernst Stuhlinger",caption:"Ernst Stuhlinger · NASA Marshall Space Flight Center · public domain"},
    sections:[
      {id:"education",title:"Physics before the war",html:`<p>Stuhlinger was born on 19 December 1913 in Niederrimbach, Württemberg. He studied physics at the University of Tübingen and completed his doctorate in 1936, then worked in cosmic-ray and nuclear physics in Berlin.</p><p>That formation distinguished him from engineers whose careers began entirely inside weapons development. Radiation, particles, instrumentation, and the upper atmosphere already belonged to his scientific world before the rocket became their carrier.</p>`},
      {id:"war",title:"Army service and Peenemünde",html:`<p>Drafted in 1941, Stuhlinger served on the eastern front and was wounded. He was transferred to Peenemünde in 1943, where his physics and electrical knowledge were applied to guidance and control.</p><p>The transfer returned him to technical work but did not remove him from the war. Peenemünde's research fed a ballistic-weapon program supported by the wider coercive economy of the Reich.</p>`},
      {id:"science",title:"A scientific program after victory",html:`<p>After victory, Stuhlinger argues that rockets should carry instruments as well as warheads and political prestige. Early satellites study the upper atmosphere, solar activity, cosmic radiation, communications, and Earth observation.</p><p>Instrument requirements alter vehicle design: payloads need clean electrical power, thermal stability, precise pointing, data storage, telemetry bandwidth, and predictable orbits. Scientific users therefore become participants in program architecture rather than passengers given whatever mass remains.</p>`},
      {id:"medicine",title:"Radiation and space medicine",html:`<p>Stuhlinger's radiation background gives him a central role in defining the hazards of crewed flight. Dosimetry, shielding, solar events, weightlessness, and closed-environment physiology become coordinated research fields.</p><p>This work links laboratories to the astronaut corps and later to long-duration stations. The state publicizes healthy crews and successful records, while the underlying institution depends on candid reporting of fatigue, exposure, and failure.</p>`},
      {id:"electric",title:"Ion and nuclear-electric propulsion",html:`<p>Stuhlinger becomes the program's leading advocate of electric propulsion. Ion engines offer very low thrust but high propellant efficiency, making them suited to long missions, station keeping, and unmanned probes rather than launch from Earth.</p><p>He couples the concept to large electrical sources, including nuclear-electric systems. The combination offers a route to cargo movement and deep-space science after chemical propulsion has performed the high-thrust work of launch and departure.</p>`},
      {id:"institutions",title:"Science within the mature program",html:`<p>Stuhlinger's laboratories define standards for payload review, calibration, data access, and the relationship between mission operations and scientific investigators. He protects exploratory work from being measured only by launch count.</p><p>His institutional counterpart is <a href='kurt-debus.html'>Kurt Debus</a>, whose range organization makes flight repeatable; his technical counterparts include <a href='helmut-groettrup.html'>Helmut Gröttrup</a> in guidance and <a href='krafft-ehricke.html'>Krafft Ehricke</a> in long-range expansion strategy.</p>`},
      {id:"succession",title:"The post-von-Braun division",html:`<p>When von Braun leaves daily direction in 1977, no single successor receives his full authority. <a href='eberhard-rees.html'>Eberhard Rees</a> manages the program, Ehricke supplies much of its public lunar and planetary argument, and Stuhlinger leads science and advanced propulsion.</p><p>The division gives Stuhlinger more influence without making him the program's sole political representative. Scientific priorities must still compete with military requirements, prestige missions, industrial schedules, and the imperial geography supporting launch and energy infrastructure.</p>`},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-f4l8t writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 --><p>Stuhlinger's exact later offices, retirement, advisory work, health, and death remain open. Compatibility with the chronology is not enough to make the historical death date a setting event.</p><p>His lasting achievement is to establish that a space program is more than transportation. German vehicles become laboratories, observatories, medical environments, and propulsion experiments, creating the scientific depth required for stations, probes, and the later Mars program.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`}
    ],
    related:[{href:"space-programs.html",kicker:"Program history",label:"Space Race"},{href:"wernher-von-braun.html",kicker:"Program architect",label:"Wernher von Braun"},{href:"eberhard-rees.html",kicker:"Management successor",label:"Eberhard Rees"},{href:"krafft-ehricke.html",kicker:"Expansion counterpart",label:"Krafft Ehricke"},{href:"orbitaler-raumhafen.html",kicker:"Research platform",label:"Orbitaler Raumhafen"},{href:"marsplan-92.html",kicker:"Later destination",label:"Marsplan 92"}],
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ernst_Stuhlinger",label:"Wikipedia — pre-divergence biography and propulsion research"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dr._Ernst_Stuhlinger_(cropped).jpg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — NASA portrait and public-domain record"}],
    categories:["Ernst Stuhlinger","German physicists","Electric propulsion","Space medicine","German space program"],
    facts:[["Born","19 December 1913"],["Setting death","Open"],["Birthplace","Niederrimbach, Württemberg"],["Education","University of Tübingen, doctorate in physics, 1936"],["Wartime work","Peenemünde guidance"],["Postwar domains","Space science, medicine, advanced propulsion"],["Propulsion specialties","Ion and nuclear-electric systems"],["Post-1977 position","Scientific and advanced-propulsion leader"],["Historical American career","Discarded branch"]]
  }),

  "helmut-groettrup": germanSpacePioneerBiography({
    title:"Helmut Gröttrup",
    infoboxTitle:"Helmut Gröttrup",
    infoboxKicker:"Guidance and computing systems engineer",
    lead:"Helmut Gröttrup was a German engineer who connected missile guidance to the Reich's postwar computing and control industries. Trained in applied physics, he led remote-guidance work at Peenemünde and after victory developed inertial navigation, digital flight control, telemetry, tracking, and automated rendezvous. His later influence joined the space program to Konrad Zuse's computing tradition and helped bring Matthias Falkenberg to Wolfgang Hitler's attention.",
    canon:"Gröttrup's birth, education, early electronics work, Peenemünde guidance career, and 1944 Gestapo detention are historical. His transfer to the Soviet Union, return to West Germany, smart-card patents, and historical commercial career do not occur in this setting. He remains in the Reich and develops guidance, telemetry, and flight computing. The setting places him alongside Rees in introducing Matthias Falkenberg to Wolfgang around 1980–81. His historical death in July 1981 conflicts with that approximate introduction window and is therefore not fixed; final titles and death remain open.",
    portrait:{src:"assets/photos/german-space-pioneers/helmut-groettrup.jpg",alt:"Portrait of guidance engineer Helmut Gröttrup",caption:"Helmut Gröttrup · Ursula Gröttrup · CC BY-SA 4.0"},
    sections:[
      {id:"education",title:"Applied physics and electronics",html:`<p>Gröttrup was born on 12 February 1916 in Cologne. He studied applied physics at the Technical University of Berlin from 1935 to 1939 and completed work associated with Hans Geiger, while also gaining experience in the electronic research world around Manfred von Ardenne.</p><p>His early career joined measurement, vacuum electronics, radio, and calculation—the practical foundations of a guidance system able to sense motion, compare it with a desired path, and issue corrections in flight.</p>`},
      {id:"peenemuende",title:"Guidance at Peenemünde",html:`<p>Gröttrup entered Peenemünde in December 1939 and became responsible for remote guidance and control work under Ernst Steinhoff. The task linked gyroscopes, radio commands, onboard circuits, ground tracking, and the aerodynamic behavior of the vehicle.</p><p>Guidance was a systems problem. A mathematically correct command was useless if sensors drifted, actuators lagged, radio links failed, or telemetry could not distinguish the cause of an error.</p>`},
      {id:"arrest",title:"The 1944 arrest",html:`<p>In March 1944 the Gestapo detained Gröttrup along with von Braun and Klaus Riedel after allegations involving defeatism and talk of spacecraft rather than weapons. He was released after roughly two weeks because the program needed his technical work.</p><p>The episode revealed the engineers' privileged vulnerability: expertise could win temporary protection, but only while the regime judged it militarily indispensable.</p>`},
      {id:"discarded",title:"The discarded Soviet and smart-card careers",html:`<p>Historically, defeat took Gröttrup into the Soviet missile program and later into West German commercial electronics, where he became associated with early smart-card patents. Neither branch occurs here.</p><p>German victory keeps his career inside a continuous state aerospace establishment. The questions that historically led toward identification systems and semiconductor devices are redirected toward flight computers, encrypted telemetry, range networks, and automated spacecraft control.</p>`},
      {id:"digital",title:"Digital flight control",html:`<p>As German computers become smaller and more reliable, Gröttrup pushes guidance from specialized analog assemblies toward programmable digital control. Inertial platforms, onboard calculation, fault checking, and telemetry are designed as one information loop.</p><p>The connection makes him a bridge to <a href='konrad-zuse.html'>Konrad Zuse</a> and the wider history described in <a href='german-computing.html'>German Computing</a>. Aerospace requirements provide funding and reliability discipline; civilian computing offers components and programming methods no missile office could develop alone.</p>`},
      {id:"tracking",title:"Tracking and automated rendezvous",html:`<p>Gröttrup's group extends control beyond the vehicle. Distributed radar, radio, optical stations, and later digital networks estimate orbits and send validated commands. Automated rendezvous requires two spacecraft to share position, velocity, timing, and docking logic with little tolerance for ambiguity.</p><p>These capabilities underpin assembly of the <a href='orbitaler-raumhafen.html'>Orbitaler Raumhafen</a> and later operation of the <a href='raumwerft.html'>Raumwerft</a>. They also support military payloads, making civil orbital logistics inseparable from strategic surveillance and command.</p>`},
      {id:"falkenberg",title:"Falkenberg and the Weltnetz generation",html:`<p>Around 1980–81 Gröttrup and <a href='eberhard-rees.html'>Eberhard Rees</a> introduce <a href='matthias-falkenberg.html'>Matthias Falkenberg</a> to Wolfgang Hitler. They recognize in the younger engineer a computing system-builder who can translate incompatible machines and institutions into a national network.</p><p>The introduction connects the founding rocket generation to the <a href='weltnetz.html'>Weltnetz</a>. Gröttrup's own career had already shown that control depends as much on shared protocols and trusted data as on engines or airframes.</p>`},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Chronology and legacy",html:`<p>Gröttrup's historical death on 4 July 1981 cannot be imported without resolving the setting's approximate 1980–81 introduction of Falkenberg. His precise late office, retirement, and death therefore remain open.</p><p>His legacy is the information architecture of German flight: sensors, computers, radio links, tracking stations, and guidance software treated as one operational system. That architecture makes routine orbital construction possible and binds the space program to the Reich's broader digital transformation.</p>`}
    ],
    related:[{href:"german-computing.html",kicker:"Industrial context",label:"German Computing"},{href:"konrad-zuse.html",kicker:"Computing counterpart",label:"Konrad Zuse"},{href:"matthias-falkenberg.html",kicker:"Younger successor",label:"Matthias Falkenberg"},{href:"weltnetz.html",kicker:"Network legacy",label:"Weltnetz"},{href:"orbitaler-raumhafen.html",kicker:"Control application",label:"Orbitaler Raumhafen"},{href:"raumwerft.html",kicker:"Automated logistics",label:"Raumwerft"}],
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helmut_Gr%C3%B6ttrup",label:"Wikipedia — pre-divergence biography, guidance work, and discarded later career"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helmut_Gr%C3%B6ttrup.jpg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — Ursula Gröttrup portrait and CC BY-SA 4.0 record"}],
    categories:["Helmut Gröttrup","German engineers","Guidance systems","German computing","German space program"],
    facts:[["Born","12 February 1916"],["Birthplace","Cologne, Germany"],["Education","TH Berlin, applied physics"],["Peenemünde responsibility","Remote guidance and control"],["Detained by Gestapo","March 1944"],["Postwar domains","Inertial guidance, flight computing, telemetry"],["Computing link","Zuse tradition and Weltnetz generation"],["Historical Soviet and smart-card careers","Discarded branch"],["Setting death","Open"]]
  }),

  "arthur-rudolph": germanSpacePioneerBiography({
    title:"Arthur Rudolph",
    infoboxTitle:"Arthur Louis Hugo Rudolph",
    infoboxKicker:"Rocket-production organizer",
    lead:"Arthur Rudolph was a German mechanical engineer and production administrator whose career joined the early liquid-fuel rocket movement to series manufacture of the A-4/V-2 and, after victory, to the Reich's heavy-launch industry. His managerial skill made complex engines and stages reproducible. It also tied him directly to Mittelwerk and the murderous forced-labor system at Mittelbau-Dora, a responsibility the victorious state later concealed behind the language of production achievement.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-g5m9u writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 -->Rudolph's birth, engineering education, early rocket work, Nazi Party and SA membership, Peenemünde responsibilities, Mittelwerk management, and involvement in forced-labor production are historical. Operation Paperclip, American citizenship, work on Pershing and Saturn V, and later U.S. denaturalization proceedings belong to the discarded branch. In the setting he remains a German production organizer. His exact postwar titles, retirement, public reckoning, and death remain open; the historical death date is not imported from the discarded American branch.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/photos/german-space-pioneers/arthur-rudolph.jpg",alt:"Arthur Rudolph holding a rocket model",caption:"Arthur Rudolph · NASA Marshall Space Flight Center · public domain"},
    sections:[
      {id:"early",title:"Early life and liquid-fuel rocketry",html:`<p>Rudolph was born on 9 November 1906 in Stepfershausen and studied mechanical engineering at the Technical University of Berlin, graduating in 1930. He worked with Max Valier and the Heylandt organization on early liquid-fuel rocket engines.</p><p>The work taught him to move between experimental apparatus, industrial drawings, suppliers, tolerances, and assembly. Rudolph's importance lay less in one visionary concept than in making a difficult device manufacturable.</p>`},
      {id:"politics",title:"Nazi affiliation",html:`<p>Rudolph joined the Nazi Party in 1931 and the SA. His political commitment preceded the regime's full sponsorship of rocketry and helped place him inside the institutions that transformed an experimental field into a state weapons program.</p><p>The setting does not treat later technical success as exculpation. His career advanced through a dictatorship whose resources and coercive power were essential to the scale of production he organized.</p>`},
      {id:"peenemuende",title:"Kummersdorf and Peenemünde",html:`<p>Rudolph moved through Kummersdorf into the Peenemünde program and took responsibility for production planning. He translated the A-4 design into factory requirements, production drawings, work sequences, tooling, inspections, and supplier schedules.</p><p>That translation exposed every instability in the design. Components that functioned in a prototype had to survive imperfect materials, changing workers, transport damage, and the pressure of military output targets.</p>`},
      {id:"mittelwerk",title:"Mittelwerk and Mittelbau-Dora",html:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-c9n5q writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T18:35:00-06:00 --><p>A-4 manufacture included underground work at Mittelwerk, where Rudolph became a leading operations manager in facilities supplied by prisoners from Mittelbau-Dora. The setting does not retain the historical scale of V-2 mass bombardment.</p><p>The conditions were murderous: prisoners labored under starvation, violence, disease, and exhausting underground conditions, and prisoners died in the production complex and its associated camps. Rudolph's production authority made him part of the system that used and consumed that labor. Reduced weapon output does not make this an incidental footnote to his management record; it remains one of its central facts.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`},
      {id:"victory",title:"Victory and the absence of reckoning",html:`<p>German victory prevents the Allied investigations, Operation Paperclip screening, and eventual American proceedings that defined Rudolph's historical postwar reputation. He remains inside the industrial establishment that benefited from the Mittelwerk experience.</p><p>Official accounts isolate sabotage and quality problems from the conditions that produced them. They celebrate the decision to shift flight-critical work toward screened skilled German labor after the mid-1940s while leaving coercion embedded in resource extraction, construction, and the imperial economy.</p>`},
      {id:"production",title:"Heavy-launch production",html:`<p>Rudolph applies wartime production control to larger postwar vehicles. Engine clusters, tanks, stage structures, wiring, valves, and test records are organized into controlled configurations so that a launcher assembled in one plant matches the documentation used at the range.</p><p>He works closely with <a href='konrad-dannenberg.html'>Konrad Dannenberg</a>, who concentrates on propulsion and stage integration, and with <a href='eberhard-rees.html'>Eberhard Rees</a>, who coordinates facilities, schedules, and program-wide production.</p>`},
      {id:"labor",title:"Labor, quality, and the imperial economy",html:`<p>The mature program distinguishes between flight-critical production and the wider labor system supporting it. Sabotage risk and low reliability move final assembly and sensitive components toward privileged German technical workforces, apprenticeships, and instrumented inspection.</p><p>That technical enclave rests on less visible foundations: mined materials, concrete, transport corridors, colonial construction, and dangerous bulk work remain structured by racial hierarchy and coercion. Better quality control narrows where forced labor is used; it does not dissolve the system.</p>`},
      {id:"memory",title:"Public memory and responsibility",html:`<p>Rudolph is presented publicly as the organizer who gave German vision an industrial body. Exhibitions emphasize engine models, production diagrams, and the progression from A-4 to lunar launcher.</p><p>The prisoners of Mittelbau-Dora are absent or described through euphemisms of wartime necessity. In a victorious Reich, the suppression is durable because no external court forces personnel records and testimony into the national narrative.</p>`},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Later life and legacy",html:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-h6n2v writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 --><p>Rudolph's exact late offices, retirement, health, public standing, and death remain open. The historical American death record is comparative biography rather than a setting endpoint.</p><p>His legacy cannot be split into a clean technical achievement and an unrelated crime. The capacity to organize mass production made him valuable to the space program; the same capacity was exercised at a factory built around prisoner exploitation and mass death.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`}
    ],
    related:[{href:"konrad-dannenberg.html",kicker:"Stage counterpart",label:"Konrad Dannenberg"},{href:"eberhard-rees.html",kicker:"Program manager",label:"Eberhard Rees"},{href:"wernher-von-braun.html",kicker:"Technical architect",label:"Wernher von Braun"},{href:"space-programs.html",kicker:"Postwar program",label:"Space Race"},{href:"german-imperial-administration.html",kicker:"Imperial labor context",label:"German Imperial Administration"},{href:"germany.html",kicker:"State and memory",label:"Greater German Reich"}],
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Rudolph",label:"Wikipedia — pre-divergence biography, Mittelwerk role, and historical proceedings"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_Rudolph.jpg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — NASA portrait and public-domain record"}],
    categories:["Arthur Rudolph","German engineers","Mittelwerk","Forced labor","German space program"],
    facts:[["Born","9 November 1906"],["Setting death","Open"],["Birthplace","Stepfershausen, Germany"],["Education","TH Berlin, mechanical engineering, 1930"],["Political affiliations","Nazi Party; SA"],["Wartime responsibility","A-4 production; Mittelwerk operations"],["Central criminal context","Forced labor at Mittelbau-Dora"],["Postwar domain","Heavy-launch production"],["Historical American career","Discarded branch"]]
  }),

  "konrad-dannenberg": germanSpacePioneerBiography({
    title:"Konrad Dannenberg",
    infoboxTitle:"Konrad Dannenberg",
    infoboxKicker:"Propulsion and stage-integration engineer",
    lead:"Konrad Dannenberg was a German mechanical engineer who became one of the Reich's principal specialists in rocket propulsion and stage integration. He entered Peenemünde in 1940, worked under Walter Thiel on the A-4 engine, and after victory carried that experience into clustered engines, multi-stage launchers, lunar vehicles, and orbital-construction logistics. His task was to make stages designed by different groups function as one vehicle.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-i7p4w writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 -->Dannenberg's birth, education, early rocket enthusiasm, Nazi Party membership, army service, transfer to Peenemünde, and propulsion work under Walter Thiel are historical. Operation Paperclip and his American Redstone, Jupiter, and Saturn careers belong to the discarded branch. In the setting analogous propulsion and stage-integration work remains within the German program. His exact setting offices, retirement, later advisory work, and death remain open; the historical death date is not imported automatically.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/photos/german-space-pioneers/konrad-dannenberg.jpg",alt:"Konrad Dannenberg standing beside a rocket exhibit",caption:"Konrad Dannenberg in a historical NASA photograph · public domain"},
    sections:[
      {id:"education",title:"Education and the amateur rocket movement",html:`<p>Dannenberg was born on 5 August 1912 in Weißenfels. He studied mechanical engineering at the Technical University of Hannover and became interested in rocketry after encountering the public movement associated with Max Valier and the interwar rocket societies.</p><p>The amateur movement supplied a language of space travel, but Dannenberg's professional value came from engineering detail: combustion, feed systems, cooling, drawings, test data, and the interfaces between engine and vehicle.</p>`},
      {id:"politics",title:"Party membership and military service",html:`<p>Dannenberg joined the Nazi Party in 1932. Drafted in 1939, he served during the early western campaign before his technical background brought him into the Peenemünde establishment in 1940.</p><p>Like the other founding engineers, his opportunity was created by a regime that placed enormous military resources behind rocketry. Membership and technical service belong to the same career, not to separate public and private biographies.</p>`},
      {id:"thiel",title:"Propulsion under Walter Thiel",html:`<p>At Peenemünde, Dannenberg worked in the propulsion group under Walter Thiel. The A-4 engine required stable combustion, controlled propellant delivery, cooling, ignition, and a manufacturing design that could survive repeated test and field handling.</p><p>After Thiel was killed in the 1943 bombing of Peenemünde, Dannenberg took on greater responsibility under Walter Riedel, including work that connected final production drawings to the tested engine configuration.</p>`},
      {id:"victory",title:"The German rather than American branch",html:`<p>Defeat historically moved Dannenberg to the United States and into the Redstone, Jupiter, and Saturn programs. In this setting that institutional transfer never occurs. He remains in Germany with the test records, factories, and colleagues from which the postwar launch program grows.</p><p>The vehicle names change, but the engineering problem is analogous: increasingly large launchers require engines, tanks, structures, guidance, separation devices, and ground equipment to be developed as an integrated stack.</p>`},
      {id:"stages",title:"Stage integration",html:`<p>Dannenberg becomes the founding generation's specialist in stage interfaces. Mass estimates, vibration, bending loads, propellant residuals, ignition sequencing, separation, wiring, and telemetry must be reconciled before independently successful components can fly together.</p><p>He treats configuration control as an engineering discipline. A late valve substitution or cable reroute can alter a test result far from the component itself, so drawings, hardware, and range documentation must describe the same vehicle.</p>`},
      {id:"heavy",title:"Heavy launch and lunar vehicles",html:`<p>The satellite and lunar programs demand engine clustering and larger stages. Dannenberg's teams organize propulsion development around ground-test articles, incremental flight versions, and failure records shared with <a href='arthur-rudolph.html'>Arthur Rudolph's production organization</a>.</p><p>The first German Moon landing in 1970 depends on this conservative accumulation of interfaces as much as on von Braun's mission architecture. Each stage must preserve margins for the next while remaining producible at the required rate.</p>`},
      {id:"orbit",title:"Orbital construction logistics",html:`<p>With the <a href='orbitaler-raumhafen.html'>Orbitaler Raumhafen</a> and later <a href='raumwerft.html'>Raumwerft</a>, stage integration expands into traffic design. Launchers carry standardized modules, propellants, tools, crews, and replacement parts into an orbital supply chain.</p><p>Dannenberg's interface culture supports docking standards and cargo envelopes that allow different contractors to contribute hardware without redesigning the entire transportation system for each mission.</p>`},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-j8q4x writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 --><p>Dannenberg's precise late title, retirement, advisory work, health, and death remain open. Compatibility with the chronology is not enough to establish the historical death date in the setting.</p><p>His legacy is the connective work often hidden by photographs of complete rockets. Engines become stages, stages become launch vehicles, and launch vehicles become a repeatable logistics system because specifications and tests are made to agree across institutional boundaries.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`}
    ],
    related:[{href:"arthur-rudolph.html",kicker:"Production counterpart",label:"Arthur Rudolph"},{href:"wernher-von-braun.html",kicker:"Mission architect",label:"Wernher von Braun"},{href:"eberhard-rees.html",kicker:"Program management",label:"Eberhard Rees"},{href:"moon-landing.html",kicker:"Lunar application",label:"German Moon Landing"},{href:"orbitaler-raumhafen.html",kicker:"Orbital logistics",label:"Orbitaler Raumhafen"},{href:"raumwerft.html",kicker:"Industrial successor",label:"Raumwerft"}],
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konrad_Dannenberg",label:"Wikipedia — pre-divergence biography and propulsion career"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konrad_Dannenbery_and_R-7_165511.jpg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — NASA photograph and public-domain record"}],
    categories:["Konrad Dannenberg","German engineers","Rocket propulsion","Launch vehicles","German space program"],
    facts:[["Born","5 August 1912"],["Setting death","Open"],["Birthplace","Weißenfels, Germany"],["Education","TH Hannover, mechanical engineering"],["Political affiliation","Nazi Party, from 1932"],["Peenemünde field","A-4 propulsion"],["Postwar domain","Stage and propulsion integration"],["Major applications","Heavy launch, lunar vehicles, orbital logistics"],["Historical American career","Discarded branch"]]
  }),

  "krafft-ehricke": germanSpacePioneerBiography({
    title:"Krafft Ehricke",
    infoboxTitle:"Krafft Arnold Ehricke",
    infoboxKicker:"Astronautical strategist and planetary-expansion advocate",
    lead:"Krafft Ehricke was a German rocket engineer, astrodynamicist, and public philosopher who gave the Reich's mature space program its most expansive civil argument. A Peenemünde propulsion engineer during the war, he later championed industrial orbit, permanent lunar settlement, nuclear propulsion, resource extraction, and Mars as a long-term civilizational project. After von Braun's retirement, Ehricke became the program's leading public voice for lunar and planetary expansion.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-k9r5y writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 -->Ehricke's birth, Berlin technical education, Peenemünde propulsion work, and major astronautical concepts are historical. Operation Paperclip, his American citizenship, work for Bell and Convair, and direct authorship of the historical Centaur program belong to the discarded branch. The setting retains his interests in liquid hydrogen, nuclear propulsion, orbital industry, lunar settlement, and the Extraterrestrial Imperative within German institutions. His precise role in Marsplan 92, retirement, and death remain open; the historical death date cannot be used to confine his influence to an intellectual legacy unless separately established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/photos/german-space-pioneers/krafft-ehricke.jpg",alt:"Portrait of astronautical engineer Krafft Ehricke",caption:"Krafft Ehricke · NASA · public domain"},
    sections:[
      {id:"education",title:"Education and astronautical formation",html:`<p>Ehricke was born on 24 March 1917 in Berlin. He studied at the Technical University of Berlin, where celestial mechanics, nuclear physics, and the public legacy of the interwar space movement shaped his approach to astronautics.</p><p>He treated flight beyond Earth as both an engineering problem and a social choice. Orbits, energy, propulsion, settlement, and industry belonged to one long sequence rather than to isolated record attempts.</p>`},
      {id:"peenemuende",title:"Peenemünde propulsion work",html:`<p>Ehricke joined Peenemünde in 1942 and worked in propulsion under Walter Thiel. The wartime program gave him practical knowledge of liquid engines, staging, vehicle performance, and the organizational scale required to turn theory into flight.</p><p>It also embedded his career in a weapons establishment serving the Nazi state. The later civil philosophy grows from institutions preserved by German victory, not from a clean postwar beginning.</p>`},
      {id:"victory",title:"A German postwar branch",html:`<p>Historically, Ehricke went to the United States and helped develop advanced launch and space-station concepts. In the setting he remains within the German program, where victory supplies continuity, state resources, and access to continental launch and industrial sites.</p><p>He is more radical than von Braun about the social purpose of spaceflight. Von Braun organizes politically achievable sequences; Ehricke insists that each sequence should enlarge the permanent sphere of human activity.</p>`},
      {id:"imperative",title:"The Extraterrestrial Imperative",html:`<p>Ehricke argues that life should expand rather than accept Earth as a closed limit. He presents spaceflight not as escape from terrestrial responsibility but as the creation of new environments, resources, and forms of work.</p><p>In the Reich, that universal language carries an unresolved political contradiction. A doctrine of human expansion is promoted by an empire that denies equal humanity within its own borders and colonies. Official presentations celebrate abundance beyond Earth while avoiding who controls access to it.</p>`},
      {id:"industry",title:"Industrial orbit",html:`<p>Ehricke advocates stations that manufacture, repair, assemble, observe, and store propellant rather than merely circle Earth as laboratories. Orbital infrastructure should reduce the need to launch every completed spacecraft from the ground.</p><p>The <a href='orbitaler-raumhafen.html'>Orbitaler Raumhafen</a> and <a href='raumwerft.html'>Raumwerft</a> embody much of this argument. Standardized docks, tanks, workshops, and tugs turn orbit into a working region with its own logistics.</p>`},
      {id:"moon",title:"Lunar settlement and resources",html:`<p>For Ehricke, a lunar landing is the beginning of a program rather than its climax. He promotes permanent bases, polar prospecting, oxygen extraction, construction from local materials, astronomy, and a transport economy linking the Moon to high Earth orbit.</p><p><a href='mondhafen-sud.html'>Mondhafen Süd</a> gives institutional form to this vision. Its practical development is slower and more politically constrained than his public plans, but the settlement vocabulary becomes normal inside German strategy.</p>`},
      {id:"propulsion",title:"Nuclear propulsion and Mars",html:`<p>Ehricke supports nuclear systems for high-power orbital work and long-range missions, alongside Stuhlinger's electric-propulsion research. He treats propulsion choice as part of an economy of cargo, crew time, radiation, and reusable infrastructure.</p><p>Mars is the ultimate test of the architecture: no single launch can substitute for assembled vehicles, stores, surface systems, and an institutional willingness to plan across decades. His ideas become a principal intellectual source for <a href='marsplan-92.html'>Marsplan 92</a>.</p>`},
      {id:"succession",title:"Public leader after von Braun",html:`<p>After von Braun leaves daily direction in 1977, the program's inheritance is divided. <a href='eberhard-rees.html'>Eberhard Rees</a> controls management, <a href='ernst-stuhlinger.html'>Ernst Stuhlinger</a> leads science and advanced propulsion, and Ehricke becomes the most visible advocate of lunar and planetary expansion.</p><p>He does not inherit von Braun's complete authority. His function is to keep the long horizon politically legible while managers and laboratory directors decide what can be built.</p>`},
      {id:"death",title:"Later life and intellectual legacy",html:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-l2s6z writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 --><p>Ehricke's retirement, health, death, and personal participation in the later Mars program remain open. The historical death date is comparative biography and does not establish that he was absent from later planning.</p><p>His conceptual legacy is established regardless of that open chronology. Industrial orbit, a working Moon, extraterrestrial resources, and Mars as an institutional project remain part of German policy.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`}
    ],
    related:[{href:"wernher-von-braun.html",kicker:"Program architect",label:"Wernher von Braun"},{href:"eberhard-rees.html",kicker:"Management counterpart",label:"Eberhard Rees"},{href:"ernst-stuhlinger.html",kicker:"Science counterpart",label:"Ernst Stuhlinger"},{href:"orbitaler-raumhafen.html",kicker:"Industrial orbit",label:"Orbitaler Raumhafen"},{href:"mondhafen-sud.html",kicker:"Lunar settlement",label:"Mondhafen Süd"},{href:"marsplan-92.html",kicker:"Planetary legacy",label:"Marsplan 92"}],
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krafft_Arnold_Ehricke",label:"Wikipedia — pre-divergence biography and astronautical concepts"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krafft_Ehricke.jpg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — NASA portrait and public-domain record"}],
    categories:["Krafft Ehricke","German engineers","Astronautics","Lunar settlement","Mars exploration"],
    facts:[["Born","24 March 1917"],["Setting death","Open"],["Birthplace","Berlin, Germany"],["Education","TH Berlin"],["Peenemünde field","Propulsion, from 1942"],["Postwar domains","Orbital industry, lunar settlement, planetary strategy"],["Core doctrine","Extraterrestrial Imperative"],["Post-1977 role","Public advocate for lunar and planetary expansion"],["Historical American career","Discarded branch"]]
  }),

  "hermann-oberth": germanSpacePioneerBiography({
    title:"Hermann Oberth",
    infoboxTitle:"Hermann Julius Oberth",
    infoboxKicker:"Astronautical theorist and elder founder",
    lead:"Hermann Oberth was a Transylvanian German physicist, teacher, and rocket theorist whose books helped establish modern astronautics before the Nazi seizure of power. He mentored the generation that built Germany's wartime rockets and, after victory, became the space program's elder prophet: a ceremonial presence at major launches and a prolific advocate of stations, lunar vehicles, electric propulsion, and travel between planets.",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-m3t7a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 -->Oberth's birth in Hermannstadt, education, publications, work with Fritz Lang, mentorship of von Braun, and wartime Peenemünde service are historical. His postwar movement through West Germany and later American work belong to the discarded branch. In the setting he remains attached to the German space establishment as a theorist and ceremonial ancestor. His exact residences, formal offices, political statements after victory, retirement, and death remain open; the historical death date is not imported automatically.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/photos/german-space-pioneers/hermann-oberth.jpg",alt:"Hermann Oberth in the 1950s",caption:"Hermann Oberth in the 1950s · Mondadori / unknown photographer · public domain in Italy"},
    sections:[
      {id:"early",title:"Early life and education",html:`<p>Oberth was born on 25 June 1894 in Hermannstadt, Transylvania, into a Transylvanian Saxon family. Inspired in youth by Jules Verne, he pursued the physical problems of spaceflight when the subject remained outside respectable engineering practice.</p><p>War, medicine, physics, and changing universities interrupted a conventional academic path. The irregular formation contributed to his role as an independent theorist willing to develop an entire field before it had stable institutions.</p>`},
      {id:"books",title:"Foundational books",html:`<p>Oberth's 1923 book <em>Die Rakete zu den Planetenräumen</em> set out the physical case for liquid-fuel rockets and space travel. The larger 1929 <em>Wege zur Raumschiffahrt</em> developed the argument into vehicles, stations, trajectories, and practical uses.</p><p>The books gave the German rocket movement a technical vocabulary and a claim to seriousness. They also connected engineering calculations to a public vision of leaving Earth.</p>`},
      {id:"movement",title:"The German rocket movement",html:`<p>Oberth worked with the Verein für Raumschiffahrt and influenced younger experimenters, above all <a href='wernher-von-braun.html'>Wernher von Braun</a>. He became less a laboratory chief than a teacher whose ideas supplied direction to people better positioned to build institutions.</p><p>His association with Fritz Lang's 1929 film <em>Woman in the Moon</em> brought rocket concepts into mass culture. Technical advising, publicity, and attempted demonstration work made astronautics visible beyond engineering circles.</p>`},
      {id:"war",title:"Wartime work at Peenemünde",html:`<p>Oberth joined Peenemünde in 1941 under the alias Friedrich Hann. He reviewed patents, prepared reports, and pursued advanced concepts rather than directing the core A-4 engineering organization.</p><p>He received the War Merit Cross in 1943. His presence linked the weapons center to the prewar dream of spaceflight, but the wartime institution served ballistic attack and was sustained by the dictatorship's coercive resources.</p>`},
      {id:"victory",title:"Elder prophet of the victorious program",html:`<p>German victory prevents the geographic and institutional dispersal of the rocket team. Oberth remains inside the national lineage that official culture presents as a continuous path from early books to satellite, Moon landing, and orbital harbor.</p><p>He appears at major launches, anniversaries, lectures, and dedications as “the teacher.” The ceremonial role gives the regime a founder older than National Socialism while allowing it to claim his entire intellectual tradition as a German state achievement.</p>`},
      {id:"concepts",title:"Stations, Moon, and electric propulsion",html:`<p>Oberth continues to write about space stations, lunar vehicles, planetary travel, extraction of lunar materials, large orbital structures, and ion propulsion. Many proposals remain speculative, but they keep long-term possibilities visible before the required materials and control systems exist.</p><p>Later engineers divide these themes into practical domains: Stuhlinger develops electric propulsion, Ehricke argues for extraterrestrial industry, and von Braun sequences the vehicles and missions.</p>`},
      {id:"politics",title:"Myth, politics, and appropriation",html:`<p>Official memory uses Oberth to portray the space program as the inevitable fulfillment of German scientific culture. That narrative compresses independent clubs, Romanian and Austro-Hungarian contexts, commercial media, military sponsorship, and coerced production into a single national ascent.</p><p>Oberth's exact postwar political statements remain open. The archive distinguishes his foundational technical work from the state's appropriation of his prestige and does not infer endorsement for every policy carried out under that prestige.</p>`},
      {id:"family",title:"Family and personal losses",html:`<p>Oberth married Mathilde Hummel in 1918 and the couple had four children. Their daughter Ilse died in 1944 in an explosion at a rocket test facility, bringing the danger of the wartime program into the family itself.</p><p>The setting retains the family history unless later character material establishes a divergence.</p>`},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Later life and legacy",html:`<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-n4u8b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:01:00-06:00 --><p>Oberth's exact residence, formal standing, health, retirement, and death remain open. His historical death in Nuremberg belongs to the discarded postwar branch and is not a setting endpoint.</p><p>His principal legacy is intellectual paternity. He supplied equations, language, and legitimacy to a generation that made rocketry a state system. In the setting, survival of that system turns him into an official ancestor on a scale the historically divided postwar world never permitted.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->`}
    ],
    related:[{href:"wernher-von-braun.html",kicker:"Most famous pupil",label:"Wernher von Braun"},{href:"space-programs.html",kicker:"Fulfillment of the movement",label:"Space Race"},{href:"ernst-stuhlinger.html",kicker:"Electric-propulsion successor",label:"Ernst Stuhlinger"},{href:"krafft-ehricke.html",kicker:"Expansion philosopher",label:"Krafft Ehricke"},{href:"moon-landing.html",kicker:"Ceremonial culmination",label:"German Moon Landing"},{href:"orbitaler-raumhafen.html",kicker:"Mature architecture",label:"Orbitaler Raumhafen"}],
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermann_Oberth",label:"Wikipedia — pre-divergence biography, books, and rocket movement"},{href:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermann_Oberth_1950s.jpg",label:"Wikimedia Commons — 1950s portrait and public-domain record"}],
    categories:["Hermann Oberth","Rocket pioneers","German physicists","Astronautics","German space program"],
    facts:[["Born","25 June 1894"],["Setting death","Open"],["Birthplace","Hermannstadt, Transylvania"],["Major books","1923 and 1929"],["Public-media work","Woman in the Moon, 1929"],["Peenemünde service","From 1941"],["Program role","Elder theorist and ceremonial founder"],["Major themes","Stations, lunar vehicles, ion propulsion, planetary travel"],["Historical postwar western career","Discarded branch"]]
  })
});
